{"id": "743f1fcc-364f-4581-a521-ea6a1a7514bd", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a hypothetical question about DynamoDB GSI throttling, so I can answer directly without AWS calls. The key mechanism is that DynamoDB writes propagate synchronously to GSIs as part of the write path, so if a GSI lacks sufficient provisioned capacity, that throttling backs up into the base table's write throughput too.

Likely culprit here is a low-cardinality GSI partition key like "status" creating a hot partition that can't absorb writes, causing the whole PutItem operation to throttle even though the base table itself has capacity to spare.

